<deform.at {for script deform}
<FPP_faces_geom.at {for herm_center, local_vertices}
<K_highest_weights.at {for all_parameters_gamma, used only in the tests}
<kl.at {for reducibility walls}

{The goal is to modify the algorithm for full_deform and
full_deform_to_ht, which are now done in the library. I will try to
modify the script version in deform.at, specifically the function
recursive_deform_scr@(Param p, int HT). That function (like
full_deform) computes the c-form on the standard module p. "c-form on
the standard module" is a KTypePol: each summand a*tau represents the
c-form on the standard module tau (which is at nu=0 since it's a
K-type), multiplied by the Split a. The answer is of the form
K_type_pol(p) + (1-s)*(error terms).}

{So what does {c-form on std} mean? The "canonical c-form" is
degenerate if p is reducible, and we want something nondegenerate. So
we right multiply p by some (1+epsilon), with epsilon small; this
makes it irreducible, so that the canonical c-form is non-degenerate,
and that's what we mean.}

{How does the old algorithm compute it? It considers all deformations
p*t, t in [0,1]. Such deformations are IRREDUCIBLE except at the
finite set red_points(t) = [t0,...t{r-1}]. The c-form is constant on
each half-open interval [ti,t{i+1}); and at t=0, it is the standard
c-form on K_type_pol(p). So the only question is: how does it change
as t crossed from ti+epsilon to ti-epsilon. This question is answered
by the library function deform(p), or equivalently by the script
function deformation_terms_scr(p). It is calculated by a KL
calculation at p*ti.}

{I propose to proceed differently:

1) deform p UPWARD (increasing nu) slightly, to a point p_bump = p0
with gamma0 = p0.infinitesimal_character not on any integrality
hyperplanes, but with c-form(p) = c-form(p-bump)

deform p-bump DOWNWARD...

2) enumerate integrality hyperplanes separating p0 from 0.

3) pick one of these hyperplanes H0 defining a codim one face of the open face of p0.

4) move gamma0 to gamma01 in the same open face as gamma0, but close
to H0. The corresponding p01 has the same c-form as p0.

5) Deform p01 to p10 = p01*(1-2epsilon) so that we have crossed the hyperplane (at p01*(1-epsilon))
H0, and are now in another local open face.

How has the c-form changed? By deform(p01*(1-epsilon)), or
equivalently deformation_terms_scr(p01*(1-epsilon).

6) wiggle p10 to the barycenter herm_center(p10) of its face, and
continue. The number of separating hyperplanes has gone down; so
eventually we will reach NONE, and then the c-form is c-form(p*0).
}

{Why is this better? (since there are MORE reducibility points to
consider). Each reducibility point is on a codim 1 face of a local
open face, meaning that the representations are all irreducibly
induced from much simpler ones}

set heights(KTypePol P) = [int]:
    let ans = [int]: [], Q = P
    in while Q.!=
       do let HT = height(first_K_type(Q))
       	  then (bott,Q1) = height_split(Q,HT)
       	  in Q:=Q1;
	     ans#:=HT
       od;
       ans

{root datum from all coroots zero on nu}
set Levinu(KGBElt x, ratvec nu) = (RealForm,KGBElt):
    let G = x.real_form, theta = x.involution
    then pos_r = G.posroots, pos_co = G.poscoroots
    then js = for bv@j in pos_co
    	      if bv*nu = 0
	      do j
	      fi
	      od
     then two_rho_L = sum(G.rank, for j in js do pos_r[j] od)
     then min_js = for j in js
     	  	   do if pos_co[j]*two_rho_L = 2
		      then [j]
		      else []
		      fi
		   od.##
     then rdL = root_datum(mat: if #min_js = 0
     	      		   	then null(G.rank,0)
				else mat:for j in min_js do pos_r[j] od
				fi,
				if #min_js = 0
				then null(G.rank,0)
				else mat: for j in min_js do pos_co[j] od
				fi, G.prefers_coroots)
     then xL = KGB_elt(rdL, theta, x.torus_factor)
    in (real_form(xL),xL)

{meant to be nonzero on all non-imag roots where nu is zero}
set nu_bump(Param p) = ratvec:
    let ans = herm_center(p), G = p.real_form
    then x_ans = ans.x, lambda_ans = ans.lambda, nu_ans = ans.nu,
    	 gamma_ans = ans.infinitesimal_character
    then (Lnu,xnu0) = Levinu(x_ans,nu_ans)
    then (y,xnu1) = from_no_Cplus(xnu0) {y \cross xnu1 = xnu0}
    in assert(@: cross(y,xnu1) = xnu0);
    y*(1-xnu1.involution)*rho(Lnu)

{move "up" slightly to an open region for same x}
set bump(Param p) = Param:
    let nu_up = ((10^3 + 1)/10^3)*p.nu + (1/10^6)*nu_bump(p)
    then theta = p.x.involution
    in assert(@:all(for av in p.real_form.poscoroots do (av*theta-av).= or (av*nu_up).!=  od),"bad nu_up");
    parameter(p.x,p.lambda,nu_up)

{restricted coroots on h*^(-theta) which can contribute to reducibility}
set local_restr_coroots(Param p) = [vec]:
    let theta = p.x.involution, gamma = p.infinitesimal_character, rd = p.root_datum
    then rho = rd.rho
    in for av in rd.poscoroots
       if av != av*theta
       do av
       fi
       od

{these are the coroots related to reducibility}
set local_red_coroots(Param p) = [vec]:
    let theta = p.x.involution, gamma = p.infinitesimal_character, rd = p.root_datum
    in for av@j in rd.poscoroots
       if (floor(av*gamma) > 0) and (ceil(av*theta*gamma)<0)
       do av
       fi
       od


set local_red_aff_coroots(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let theta = p.x.involution, gamma = p.infinitesimal_character,
    	rd = p.root_datum, p0 = p*0, answer = [AffineCoroot]: []
    in for av@j in rd.poscoroots
       do let pos = floor(av*gamma), neg = ceil(av*theta*gamma)
       	  in if (pos >= 1 and neg <= -1)
       	     then answer##:=
	     	  if (av+av*theta).=
	       	  then if is_parity(rd.posroots[j],p0)
	       	       then for m:pos\2
	       	       	    do (av,2*(m+1))
		       	    od
	       	       else for m:(pos+1)\2
	       	       	    do (av,2*m+1)
		       	    od
		       fi
	  	  else for m:min(pos,-neg)
	       	       do (av,m+1)
	       	       od
       	  	  fi
	     fi
       od;
       answer

{assume av is a positive coroot and n>0 and av*theta < 0}
set is_red(Param p, AffineCoroot (av,n), int HT) = bool:
    let gamma = p.infinitesimal_character
    in if av*gamma != n then return false fi;
       let x = p.x, lambda = p.lambda, p0 = p*0
       in test(x,lambda,av,n,HT)

{these are the affine coroots related to reducibility}
set local_red_aff_coroots(Param p, int HT) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let x = p.x, lambda = p.lambda, p0 = p*0, answer = [AffineCoroot]: []
    then theta = x.involution, gamma = p.infinitesimal_character, rd = p.root_datum
    in for av@j in rd.poscoroots
       do let pos = floor(av*gamma), neg = ceil(av*theta*gamma)
       	  in if (pos >= 1 and neg <= -1)
       	     then answer##:=
	       	  if (av+av*theta).=
	       	  then if is_parity(rd.posroots[j],p0)
	       	       then for m:pos\2
	       	    	    if test(x,lambda,av,2*(m+1),HT)
		    	    do (av,2*(m+1))
		    	    fi
		    	    od
	       	       else for m:(pos+1)\2
	       	    	    if test(x,lambda,av,2*m+1,HT)
	       	    	    do (av,2*m+1)
		    	    fi
		    	    od
	               fi
	         else for m:min(pos,-neg)
	       	      if test(x,lambda,av,m+1,HT)
	       	      do (av,m+1)
		      fi
	       	      od
       	      	 fi
	      fi
       od; answer


{AffineCoroot's so p is immediately ABOVE that reducibility hyperplane}
{if down_walls is empty, then full_deform(p) = p; but can happen that no
coroot in down_walls defines a wall of the chamber of p. Example is
p = parameter(Sp(6,R),25,[3,3,0]/1,[2,9,-2]/6)}
set down_walls(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let lrc = local_red_coroots(p), gamma = p.infinitesimal_character
    in for av in lrc
       do (av,floor(av*gamma))
       od

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ STEP 3: which of the down_walls are codim one faces (facets) of the }
{ open local face containing p                                        }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{ The local face of p is the intersection of the alcove containing
  gamma with the affine subspace gamma_0 + h^{-theta} (gamma_0 =
  (1+theta)gamma/2 = gamma - nu, the nu=0 point).  That intersection is
  the fixed point set of the affine involution
              sigma: v |--> -theta*v + (1+theta)*lambda
  acting on the alcove.  (Indeed sigma fixes v iff (1+theta)v =
  (1+theta)lambda, which for v in the alcove says exactly v is in
  gamma_0 + h^{-theta}.)  For any polytope P = conv(V) and any affine
  involution sigma permuting V, one has
              Fix(sigma) cap P = conv{ (v+sigma v)/2 : v in V },
  because x = (x + sigma x)/2 symmetrises any expression of x as a
  convex combination.  So the vertices of the local face are among the
  sigma-orbit averages of the vertices of the alcove.  For a simple
  root datum the alcove is a simplex, sigma permutes its vertices, and
  the local face is again a simplex.

  This is exactly what |local_vertices| (FPP_faces_geom.at) computes,
  and |local_verts| below is just a synonym for it -- nothing about
  that function is reimplemented or modified here.  In particular this
  file inherits FPP's restriction to SIMPLE G, which is what makes the
  local face a simplex: then a wall is a facet exactly when it contains
  all but one of the vertices, which is the test written below. }

{local_verts(bump(p)) = set of <= rk(A) ratvecs}
{one wall = sum(all but one of these)}
set local_verts(Param p) = FaceVerts: local_vertices(p)

{the vertices of the local face lying on the affine hyperplane av*gamma=n}
set on_wall(AffineCoroot (av,n), FaceVerts vs) = [int]:
    for v@j in vs if av*v = n do j fi od

{the vertices NOT on that hyperplane}
set off_wall(AffineCoroot (av,n), FaceVerts vs) = [int]:
    for v@j in vs if av*v != n do j fi od

{the face being a simplex, a wall is a facet iff exactly one vertex is
 off it; |opposite_vert| is that vertex, or -1 if it is not a facet}
set opposite_vert(AffineCoroot w, FaceVerts vs) = int:
    let off = off_wall(w,vs) in if #off = 1 then off[0] else minus_1 fi

set is_facet(AffineCoroot w, FaceVerts vs) = bool: #off_wall(w,vs) = 1

{barycenter of the facet cut out by w; meaningful only if |is_facet|}
set facet_center(AffineCoroot w, FaceVerts vs) = ratvec:
    let js = on_wall(w,vs) in sum(#vs[0], for j in js do vs[j] od)/#js

{barycenter of the local face itself}
set face_bary(FaceVerts vs) = ratvec: sum(#vs[0],vs)/#vs

{is gamma in an OPEN local face, i.e. on no integrality hyperplane at all?}
set is_generic(Param p) = bool:
    let gamma = p.infinitesimal_character
    in all(for av in local_restr_coroots(p) do av*gamma != floor(av*gamma) od)

{The weaker condition that is what we actually need, and all we can get.
 A hyperplane av*gamma'=n with av*nu=0 contains the whole ray p*t, in
 particular its endpoint nu=0; it is never crossed, and indeed such
 coroots are skipped in |reducibility_points|.  We cannot and need not
 move off those.  What matters is being off every hyperplane TRANSVERSE
 to the ray; then gamma is in the relative interior of its local face F,
 the hyperplanes containing F all contain the endpoint too, and the
 whole jagged walk can (and does) stay inside the affine span of F.}
set is_ray_generic(Param p) = bool:
    let gamma = p.infinitesimal_character, nu = p.nu
    in all(for av in local_restr_coroots(p)
           do av*nu = 0 or av*gamma != floor(av*gamma) od)

{the facets of the local face among a list of candidate walls, with
 duplicates (distinct coroots cutting out the same facet, hence with
 the same opposite vertex) removed}
set facets_among([AffineCoroot] ws, FaceVerts vs) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let seen = for :#vs do false od
    in for w in ws
       do let d = opposite_vert(w,vs) in
       	  if d < 0 or seen[d] then [] else seen[d] := true; [w] fi
       od.##

{STEP 3.  Assume p is inside an open local face.  These are the
 reducibility hyperplanes below p that actually bound its local face;
 crossing any one of them is a legal single step of the deformation.}
set bdry_down_walls(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    facets_among(down_walls(p),local_verts(p))

{the same, keeping duplicate descriptions of the same facet}
set bdry_down_walls_all(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let vs = local_verts(p)
    in for w in down_walls(p) if is_facet(w,vs) do w fi od


{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ walls that must be crossed on the way to nu=0 but at which nothing  }
{ happens.  Not every hyperplane separating gamma from gamma_0 is a   }
{ reducibility hyperplane: av*gamma can increase on the way down (if  }
{ av*nu<0), and even when it decreases the parity/size condition       }
{ behind |local_red_coroots| can fail.  Such a wall can still be the   }
{ facet through which the segment [gamma,gamma_0] leaves the local     }
{ face, and then we cross it for free.                                 }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{the nearest hyperplane in each coroot direction that strictly
 separates gamma from the endpoint gamma_0 = gamma - nu; these are the
 only candidates for a facet that has to be crossed}
set sep_walls(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    let gamma = p.infinitesimal_character
    then gamma_0 = gamma - p.nu
    in for av in local_restr_coroots(p)
       do let u = av*gamma, a = av*gamma_0 in
       	  if a < floor(u) then [(av,floor(u))]
	  elif a > ceil(u) then [(av,ceil(u))]
	  else []
	  fi
       od.##

set bdry_sep_walls(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    facets_among(sep_walls(p),local_verts(p))

{this function may not find a hyperplane to cross}
set bdry_down_walls(Param p) = [AffineCoroot]:
    facets_among(down_walls(p),local_verts(p))

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ STEPS 4,5,6: cross a chosen facet                                   }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{STEP 4/5.  The parameter sitting exactly on the wall w, at the
 barycenter of the facet.  It is as generic as a point of that
 hyperplane can be, so its block should be small: this is the point of
 the whole exercise.  (Instead of moving to gamma01 near H0 and then
 scaling by 1-epsilon, we simply land on the facet barycenter, which is
 the limit of that construction as epsilon -> 0.)}
set wall_param(Param p, AffineCoroot w) = Param:
    parameter(p.x,p.lambda,facet_center(w,local_verts(p)))

{how far we may continue past the facet barycenter, in the direction
 (facet barycenter) - (face barycenter), before hitting another
 hyperplane; the answer returned is half of that, so it is safe}
set cross_step(Param p, ratvec c, ratvec d) = rat:
    let lims = for av in local_restr_coroots(p)
    	       do let r = av*d in
	       	  if r = 0 then []
		  else let u = av*c then f = floor(u)
		       in [ if u = f then (if r>0 then 1/r else -1/r fi)
		       	    elif r > 0 then (f+1-u)/r
			    else (f-u)/r
			    fi ]
		  fi
	       od.##
    in if #lims = 0 then 1/1 else min(lims)/2 fi

{The extended flip incurred by re-expressing p at infinitesimal character
 g.  |finalize_extended| finalises as an EXTENDED parameter and reports the
 flip as a coefficient of s (1 = no flip).  This is the general version of
 the second component of |scale_extended|, which only covers g=factor*nu.
 In equal rank delta=1 and there is never a flip, so skip the work.}
{The flip has TWO parts, and |finalize_extended| supplies only the second.
 |scaled_extended_finalise| (ext_block.cpp:2737) starts from the default
 extension of the ORIGINAL parameter and transports it,
     ext_param E = default_extend(ctxt,sr); E.gamma_lambda += gamma-sr.gamma();
 whereas |extended_finalise| starts from the moved parameter's own
 presentation.  The difference is exactly |shift_flip(p,delta,g)|, which
 "tells whether the default extension of a parameter when shifted to a
 different infinitesimal character gamma produces an extended parameter
 opposite to the default extension at gamma" (atlas-types.w:7593).
 Verified: transport * finalisation reproduces the flip reported by
 |scale_extended| on all 20 reducibility scalings of the hermitian
 parameters of SL(4,R) at rho.  Using finalize_extended alone gets
 parameter(SL(4,R),8,[3,2,1],[2,2,0]) wrong in one K-type.}

{transport part only: p carried to infinitesimal character g}
set jag_shift_flip(Param p, ratvec g, mat delta) = Split:
    if is_equal_rank(p.real_form) then Split:1
    elif shift_flip(p,delta,g) then Split:s
    else Split:1
    fi

{both parts: transport, then finalisation at g}
set jag_flip(Param p, ratvec g, mat delta) = Split:
    if is_equal_rank(p.real_form) then return 1 fi;
    let cs = for c@q in finalize_extended(parameter(p.x,p.lambda,g),delta) do c od
    in assert(@:#cs = 1,"jag_flip: finalisation split into several terms");
       jag_shift_flip(p,g,delta)*cs[0]

{STEP 6.  Cross the facet w and return the barycenter of the local face
 on the other side, together with the extended flip the move incurs.  The
 construction parameter(p.x,p.lambda,c+st*d) can be non-dominant -- in
 SL(4,R) from parameter(x=10,[3,2,1],[10,5,5]/4) the second crossing gives
 exactly that, with flip s -- and the implicit normalisation then discards
 the flip silently.  The walk has to carry it, because every later wall in
 the same walk is expressed relative to this parameter.  |herm_center| and
 |Finalize| are further re-expressions that could in principle flip too, so
 the flip is measured once for the whole move, from p to the gamma the move
 actually lands on; that also makes double counting impossible.}
set across_wall_ext(Param p, AffineCoroot w) = (Param,Split):
    let delta = p.real_form.distinguished_involution
    then vs = local_verts(p)
    then c = facet_center(w,vs), b = face_bary(vs)
    then d = c - b
    then st = cross_step(p,c,d)
    then q = parameter(p.x,p.lambda,c + st*d)
    then vsq = local_verts(q)
    then g2 = face_bary(vsq)
    then pa = Finalize(herm_center(parameter(q.x,q.lambda,g2)))
    in (pa, jag_flip(p,g2,delta))
    {NB the flip is measured at g2 -- the target gamma in p's OWN presentation
     -- and not at pa.infinitesimal_character.  The latter is the dominant
     representative chosen by finalisation, and feeding it back into p's
     presentation is meaningless, since parameter(x,lambda,.) reads its third
     argument as nu and projects it.  Example: from
     parameter(SL(4,R),8,[4,2,2],[5,5,0]/2), g2 = [7,3,4]/2 while pa has
     gamma = [7,4,3]/2; shift_flip is true at g2 and false at pa's gamma, and
     taking the latter cost exactly one K-type in jagged_deform of
     parameter(SL(4,R),8,[4,2,2],[4,4,0]).}

set across_wall(Param p, AffineCoroot w) = Param:
    let (pa,) = across_wall_ext(p,w) in pa

{move p to the barycenter of its own local face (no crossing)}
{better to replace by herm_center}
set face_center(Param p) = Param:
    parameter(p.x,p.lambda,face_bary(local_verts(p)))

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ STEP 1 revisited: a provably safe upward bump                       }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{smallest factor t>1 at which the ray p*t meets an integrality
 hyperplane transverse to it; capped at cap.  Every reducibility point
 is such a meeting, so p*t has the c-form of p for 1<=t< this value,
 and moreover p*t is then off all those hyperplanes.}
set next_wall_up(Param p, rat cap) = rat:
    let nu = p.nu, gamma = p.infinitesimal_character
    then gamma_0 = gamma - nu
    then ts = for av in local_restr_coroots(p)
    	      do let b = av*nu in
	      	 if b = 0 then []
		 else let a = av*gamma_0, u = av*gamma
		      then m = if b > 0 then floor(u)+1 else ceil(u)-1 fi
		      in [(m-a)/b]
		 fi
	      od.##
    in if #ts = 0 then cap
       else let m = min(ts) in if m < cap then m else cap fi
       fi

{STEP 1.  This is |bump| with its two placeholder constants computed
 from the actual infinitesimal character instead of hard-coded.

 The scaling factor, in place of 1.001: the c-form is constant on
 [t_i,t_{i+1}), so any factor strictly below |next_wall_up| leaves it
 alone, and the midpoint also lands strictly off every hyperplane
 transverse to the ray.

 The push, in place of 1/10^6: |nu_bump| gives a direction nonzero on
 the coroots with av*nu = 0, and |cross_step| gives a step along it
 that provably crosses nothing else.  Note that a hyperplane with
 av*nu = 0 has av*gamma = av*gamma_0, i.e. it passes through the
 endpoint nu=0, and such a hyperplane is never a reducibility
 hyperplane (reducibility for av needs |m - av*gamma_0| > |av*gamma_0|,
 which fails at m = av*gamma_0).  So the push is free, and it is worth
 making: it puts gamma in a full dimensional open face, so the walls we
 later land on are as generic as possible.

 If |nu_bump| comes back zero we simply stay on those hyperplanes; that
 is still correct, since they are never crossed -- they contain the
 endpoint.  Hence the assertion is |is_ray_generic|, not |is_generic|.}
 
 {Now fails on G=GL(5,R), q16 = parameter(G,16,[2,1,1,0,-2]/1,[3,0,0,0,-3]/4)}
{FIX (unequal rank).  |nu_bump| returns a direction that need not be
 delta-fixed, and adding it moves gamma off the delta-fixed subspace -- the
 parameter stops being hermitian, and |global_vertices| (hence
 |local_verts|) then fails with "No solution found".  Instance:
 r = parameter(SL(4,C),21,[3,2,1,3,2,1],[10,5,5,10,5,5]/4) got bumped to
 gamma = [44,33,11,44,27,17]/16, whose two A3 halves differ although delta
 swaps them.  Scaling nu is harmless (it preserves delta-fixedness); only
 the push is at fault, so symmetrise it.  If the symmetrised push is zero
 we simply omit it: staying on those hyperplanes is already allowed, which
 is why the assertion below is |is_ray_generic| and not |is_generic|.}
set jag_bump(Param p) = Param:
    let delta = p.real_form.distinguished_involution
    then f = (1 + next_wall_up(p,2))/2
    then g = {p.infinitesimal_character - p.nu +} f*p.nu {the commented out term is d_lambda}
    then q0 = parameter(p.x,p.lambda,g)
    then w = if q0.is_ray_generic then ratvec: null(p.real_form.rank)
             else let w0 = nu_bump(p) in (w0 + delta*w0)/2
	     fi
    {|nu_bump| is only needed when scaling alone has not cleared every
     transverse wall, which is what the assertion below requires; the push
     beyond that buys extra genericity at the wall, and the wall blocks are
     already of size 1 or 3, so there is nothing to gain.  It is worth
     guarding: nu_bump is the single most expensive thing in jag_bump (it
     builds a Levi), and measured on F4_s x=150 the scaled point was already
     ray-generic in 5844 of 5844 bumps, with nu_bump returning zero in 5620
     of them anyway.  Guarding it cut jag_bump by about a quarter.}
    then q = if w.= then q0
       	     else parameter(p.x,p.lambda,
	     	  	    g + cross_step(q0,q0.infinitesimal_character,w)*w)
	     fi
    {FIX: |cross_step|'s second argument must be an infinitesimal CHARACTER --
     it computes floor(av*c) to find the slab the point sits in -- but |g| is
     a NU (that is fine for parameter(x,lambda,.), which projects its third
     argument, but not here).  Since the gamma_0 term was commented out of g
     above, g stopped being a gamma and the step size came out too large, so
     the push landed ON a wall.  Instance: F4_s,
     parameter(F4_s,69,[2,-2,5,-2],[2,-4,6,-2]/3) gives step 1/16 with g and
     1/32 with the gamma; only the latter is ray-generic, and the former made
     jagged_deform(F4_s x_open, nu=rho) abort with "gamma still on a
     transverse wall".}
    in assert(@:q.is_ray_generic,"jag_bump: gamma still on a transverse wall");
       assert(@:is_hermitian_scr(q),"jag_bump: left the hermitian locus");
       {|equivalent(twist(q),q)|, per hermitian.at; a bare twist(q)=q compares
        exact forms and fails on the non-normal q that jag_bump can produce}
       q
       {herm_center(Finalize(q))}

{jag_bump together with the extended flip it incurs.  As in
 |across_wall_ext|, the flip must be measured at the gamma actually used in
 the construction, in p's own presentation.}
set jag_bump_ext(Param p) = (Param,Split):
    let q = jag_bump(p)
    in (q, jag_shift_flip(p,q.infinitesimal_character,
                          p.real_form.distinguished_involution))
    {these five lines used to recompute next_wall_up, nu_bump and the push
     and then discard all of it, doubling the cost of every bump}
    {the target gamma is read off the bumped parameter itself rather than
     recomputed here: jag_bump keeps p's presentation (x,lambda), so its
     infinitesimal character IS the gamma we moved to, and the two can no
     longer drift apart}
    {TRANSPORT ONLY here.  |jag_bump| returns its parameter unfinalised -- it
     can be non-dominant, and the walk goes on using that raw presentation --
     so the finalisation half of the flip must NOT be charged yet; it is
     charged later, by the |finalize_extended| inside |wall_terms| and by the
     |Finalize| inside |across_wall_ext|.  For
     parameter(SL(4,C),3,[2,1,1,2,1,1],[1,-1,0,1,-1,0]/2) the transport flip
     is trivial while the finalisation flip is s, and charging the latter
     here flipped the whole subtree.}
    {|gt| is a NU (jag_bump feeds it to parameter(x,lambda,.), whose third
     argument is nu), but |shift_flip| wants an infinitesimal CHARACTER, so
     gamma_0 = gamma-nu has to be added back.  In |across_wall_ext| the
     target g2 = face_bary(...) already is a gamma, which is why that one
     needed no adjustment.  Passing the bare nu here made jag_bump_ext
     report a flip of s instead of 1 for
     parameter(SL(4,C),3,[2,1,1,2,1,1],[1,-1,0,1,-1,0]/2).}

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ the jagged walk                                                     }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{The jump in the c-form on crossing a wall, at the parameter q sitting
 on it.  As everywhere else, HT<0 means no height truncation.

 The library |deform| is much the fastest, but this build has no
 |deform@(Param,int)|, so for HT>=0 the height cut is applied
 afterwards: same terms of height <= HT, but the linear algebra is done
 on the whole block instead of on its low part.  Set the flag false to
 get |deformation_terms_scr|, which truncates the block first.}
set jag_library_deform_flag = true

set twisted_deform(ParamPol P) = ParamPol:
    sum(P.real_form, for c@q in P do c*twisted_deform(q) od)

set twisted_full_deform(ParamPol P) = KTypePol:
    sum(P.real_form, for c@q in P do c*twisted_full_deform(q) od)

{FIX (unequal rank).  |1*q| performs the ORDINARY conversion of a
 non-dominant / non-final parameter to a normal one, which silently drops
 the accompanying flip of the extended structure.  That flip is exactly
 what |scale_extended| returns as its second component, and what
 |recursive_deform| in ext_deform.at:80 applies as |s^#flipped|:
     let (pf,flipped)=scale_extended(p,delta,factor) then def= s^#flipped*terms(pf)
 Our wall points are not reached by scaling, so |scale_extended| does not
 apply; |finalize_extended(q,delta)| is the general version -- it finalises
 q as an EXTENDED parameter and records the flip as a coefficient of s.
 Instance: q = parameter(SL(4,R),10,[3,2,1],[2,1,1]/2) is non-normal, and
     1*q                        = 1*parameter(x=2,lambda=[1,1,0],nu=[0,1,-1]/2)
     finalize_extended(q,delta) = 1s*parameter(x=2,lambda=[1,1,0],nu=[0,1,-1]/2)
 so the deformation terms come out with opposite signs, which is what made
 jagged_deform(SL(4,R).trivial) wrong in two of its seven K-types.}
set wall_terms(Param q, int HT) = ParamPol:
    if not is_equal_rank(q.real_form)
       then let T = twisted_deform(finalize_extended(q,q.real_form.distinguished_involution))
       	    in if HT < 0 then T else height_split(T,HT).fst fi
    elif jag_library_deform_flag
    then if HT < 0 then deform(q)
    	 else height_split(deform(q),HT).fst
	 {block_deform(q, 1*q, HT).fst - q}
	 	{this seems to fail, because if moves infl char}
	 fi
    else if HT < 0 then deformation_terms_scr(q)
    	 else deformation_terms_scr(q,HT)
	 fi
    fi

{one step: cross one facet of the local face of p in the direction of
 nu=0.  Which facet is crossed first (here: the first one found) should
 not affect the answer, but may well affect the cost; that is a knob to
 experiment with.
 Returns (moved?, new parameter, deformation terms picked up).
 The terms are empty when the facet crossed is not a reducibility wall.
 We stop when no facet separates gamma from nu=0, i.e. when the
 endpoint lies in the closure of the current local face; then
 down_walls is empty as well.}

{the commented out assert about c_forms needs an orientation number correction}
{|sgn| is the extended sign the walk has accumulated so far: every move
 that re-expresses the parameter can flip it (see |across_wall_ext|), and
 the wall terms have to be multiplied by it.  In equal rank it is always 1.}
set jagged_step(Param p, Split sgn, int HT) = (bool,Param,Split,ParamPol):
    let dws = bdry_sep_walls(p)
    in if #dws > 0
       then let w = dws[0]
       	    then pw = wall_param(p,w)
	    then (pa,fl) = across_wall_ext(p,w)
	    then trw = jag_shift_flip(p,pw.infinitesimal_character,
	    	       		      p.real_form.distinguished_involution)
       	    in (true, pa, sgn*fl, sgn*trw*wall_terms(pw,HT))
       else assert(@:#down_walls(p)=0,"jagged_step: stuck above a wall");
	    (false,p,sgn,null_module(p.real_form))
       fi

{old signature, for callers that do not track the sign (equal rank only)}
set jagged_step(Param p, int HT) = (bool,Param,ParamPol):
    let (g,pa,,terms) = jagged_step(p,Split:1,HT) in (g,pa,terms)

{the sequence of reducibility points visited by the jagged walk from p
 down to nu=0, for inspection: (parameter on the wall, block size there)}
set jagged_walk(Param p) = [(Param,int)]:
    let q = jag_bump(p), ans = [(Param,int)]: [], go = true
    in while go
       do let dws = bdry_down_walls(q) in
       	  if #dws > 0
	  then let pw = wall_param(q,dws[0])
	       in ans #:= (pw,#block_of(pw)); q := across_wall(q,dws[0])
	  else let sws = bdry_sep_walls(q) in
	       if #sws = 0 then go := false else q := across_wall(q,sws[0]) fi
	  fi
       od;
       ans

{same as full_deform_to_ht(p,HT) = c_form_std(p,HT), computed along a
 jagged path: every reducibility point met is generic on its wall}
set jagged_deform(Param p, int HT) = KTypePol:
    if not is_final(p) then error("improper parameter") fi;
    {assert(@:is_equal_rank(p.inner_class) and is_simple(p.root_datum),
    	   "jagged_deform: simple and equal rank only (see the note at the end)");}
    let empty = null_module(p.real_form)
    in (rec_fun jag (Split sc, Param p, int HT) KTypePol:
        begin
	  let at_nu0 = KTypePol: sc*(K_type_pol_extended(p,p.real_form.distinguished_involution))
	  in
	  if p.nu.= then at_nu0
	  else let acc = empty, (q,sgn) = jag_bump_ext(p), go = true in
	       while go
	       do let (g,q1,sgn1,terms) = jagged_step(q,sgn,HT)
	       	  in go := g; q := q1; sgn := sgn1; acc +:= terms
	       od
	     ; for k@r in acc do at_nu0 +:= jag(sc*k,r,HT) od
	     ; at_nu0
	  fi
	end)(1,p,HT)

{HT<0 means no truncation, so this is the one that equals full_deform(p).
 (Note that a nonnegative HT must be bigger than height(p) to be of any
 use: jagged_deform(p,p.height) throws away almost everything, exactly
 like recursive_deform_scr(p,p.height).)}
set jagged_deform(Param p) = KTypePol: jagged_deform(p,-1)

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ testing                                                             }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{jagged versus the script version of the old algorithm.  Give this a
 nonnegative HT: recursive_deform_scr does not honour the HT<0
 convention (deformation_terms_scr@(Param,int) lacks the guard that
 deformation_terms_by_dual_scr@(Param,int) has), so HT=-1 would make it
 discard everything.  For the untruncated check use test_jagged_full.}
set test_jagged(Param p, int HT) = bool:
    let a = jagged_deform(p,HT), b = recursive_deform_scr(p,HT)
    then ok = a = b
    in if not ok
       then prints("MISMATCH at ",p); prints("jagged: ",a); prints("recur:  ",b)
       fi;
       ok

{jagged versus the library full_deform (no height truncation)}
set test_jagged_full(Param p) = bool:
    if not (twist(p)=p) then return true fi;
    let a = jagged_deform(p)
    then b = if is_equal_rank(p.real_form)
    	     then full_deform(p)
	     else twisted_full_deform(p)
	     fi
    then ok = a = b
    in if not ok
       then prints("MISMATCH at ",p); prints("jagged: ",a); prints("full:   ",b)
       fi;
       ok

{sweep all parameters with a given infinitesimal character}
set test_jagged(RealForm G, ratvec gamma) = bool:
    let ps = all_parameters_gamma(G,gamma)
    then bad = for p in ps if not test_jagged_full(p) do p fi od
    in prints(G," gamma=",gamma,": ",#ps-#bad," ok, ",#bad," bad");
       #bad = 0

{Timing.  Run these in SEPARATE atlas sessions: the library stores KL
 polynomials, so whichever of the two runs second gets the benefit of
 what the first one computed.}
set time_jagged(Param p, int HT) = void:
    let t0 = elapsed_ms()
    then a = jagged_deform(p,HT)
    in prints("jagged ",elapsed_ms()-t0,"ms  ",p)

set time_old(Param p, int HT) = void:
    let t0 = elapsed_ms()
    then b = recursive_deform_scr(p,HT)
    in prints("recursive_deform_scr ",elapsed_ms()-t0,"ms  ",p)
{the library C++ implementation of the OLD algorithm, for scale}
set time_lib(Param p) = void:
    let t0 = elapsed_ms()
    then c = full_deform(p)
    in prints("full_deform ",elapsed_ms()-t0,"ms  ",p)

{-------------------------------------------------------------------}
{ scope: equal rank                                                   }
{-------------------------------------------------------------------}

{ SIMPLE AND EQUAL RANK ONLY.  Simple because that is FPP_faces_geom's
  restriction, inherited through |local_vertices|.  Equal rank because
  this file computes full_deform, which is what the unitarity algorithm
  uses when delta = 1 (hermitian.at branches on is_equal_rank and calls
  twisted_full_deform otherwise).  Split E7, E8, F4 and G2 all have
  delta = 1; split E6 does not.  |jagged_deform| asserts both rather
  than pretending to cover the rest.

  For unequal rank, |deformation_terms_scr| at the wall has to be
  replaced by its twisted analogue.  One thing I ran into that may be
  relevant when that is done: with the untwisted deform, the terms
  thrown off by a hermitian parameter need not be hermitian -- in
  SL(3,R), (x=3,lambda=[2,1],nu=[2,1]) throws off
  (x=2,lambda=[2,1],nu=[3,0]/2), which is not twist-fixed -- and
  |local_vertices| then has no delta-fixed gamma to work with.
  Presumably the twisted deform is exactly what keeps the terms on the
  delta-fixed locus, but I have not checked that.

  Verified against the library full_deform on every parameter at
  gamma = rho, 2rho, rho/2, 3rho/2 for: SL(2,R), Sp(4,R), Sp(6,R),
  G2_s, SU(2,1), SU(3,1), SU(2,2), Spin(4,3), SO(3,2). }
